                        "glosses": [
                            "A cicada, especially in Greece."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8ea3a30e-2011-4cf6-89dc-be0cff7cd285",
                                "sense_id": "21e85c15-6f87-3ee2-b857-b4b0170700bc",
                                "text": "[T]he Tettix or Cicada in the day time is extremely troubleſome. It is a brown inſect reſembling a chafer, with wings much longer than its body, and thin like thoſe of a fly. […] Dionyſius of Syracuſe ſignified his reſolution to burn and lay waſte the territory of a people, with whom he had a quarrel, when he ſaid, that, if they refuſed to comply with his demands, their Tettixes ſhould ſing on the ground."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1e1cb90f-8736-4a40-ba65-3d7ee1d71ba8",
                                "sense_id": "21e85c15-6f87-3ee2-b857-b4b0170700bc",
                                "text": "[T]hat Greek waste silent but for the risp of the tettix and the wash of listless wind through the spare grass."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a186c5b9-40c0-45c6-9525-987148bf3fa0",
                                "sense_id": "21e85c15-6f87-3ee2-b857-b4b0170700bc",
                                "text": "The woman who had lived on no food at all, however, recalls once more the insect into which Tithonos was transformed: the tettix, which was believed to exist without food or drink, or only on dew, or on dew and air."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
